            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The winepress primarily symbolizes divine wrath and eschatological judgment, with separate secondary meanings including agricultural abundance/blessing and God's provision in the vineyard parables.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The winepress symbolizes the vineyard's reckoning, which yields either abundant new wine for good fruit or the blood of divine wrath for wickedness.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Divine wrath and judgment, with blessing and abundance treated as a separate, alternative meaning.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A reckoning of a people's fruit, with two possible outcomes: blessing (new wine) or judgment (blood).\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_BROADENS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book elevates and unifies the consensus's separate primary (wrath) and alternative (abundance/blessing) meanings, combining them into a single overarching concept of 'reckoning' based on the quality of the vintage.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. Both the consensus and the book agree on the specific textual meanings (wrath and abundance); they differ only in how those meanings are conceptually organized.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The consensus views the negative (crushing/wrath) and positive (overflowing vats/blessing) aspects of the winepress as distinct symbols. The book refines this by synthesizing them into a unified concept of harvest-time reckoning, where the outcome depends on the nature of the fruit.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The consensus treats the positive and negative uses of the winepress as distinct, context-dependent metaphors (agricultural blessing vs. violent crushing) rather than a unified theological symbol of 'reckoning.'\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
